In article <350tru$k5r@senator-bedfellow.mit.edu>,
Seth Finkelstein <sethf@athena.mit.edu> wrote:
>
>	If I may jump in, I believe I can state the point succinctly: Is
>this an instance of "Free Speech For Me, But Not For Thee"? (after the
>title of a book on that topic by Nat Hentoff).

In my opinion, you are correct. 

I am responsible for computing services at at college in Delaware. One of 
our students is a "Chuck Whealton" who likes to stir up trouble by posting 
very offensive (but non-threatening) remarks to alt.homosexual, an alt 
group with no usage charter. As most of us know, offensive speech is 
still protected speech under U.S. law.

Mr. Riley and another user from Interlog sent me email demanding the 
removal of Chuck Whealton's account. Mr Riley also stated that he would 
be notifying the president of our college about Chuck's behaviour, which 
apparently is in the process of happening right now. (Said demands and 
threats were also publically posted in alt.homosexual, references below.)

As a fairly new usenet site, we have not yet had a test case.  Our board of
directors are quite sensitive to negative publicity.  They will not be able
to specifically censor Mr. Whealton directly, but most likely will get
quite flustered that people talk about ANY kind of sex on the Internet.
The end result will be most likely, I will be forced to close down our
newsfeed.  This is the only way they will be able to control the
situation, without arbitrarily deciding who can post what where, which is
illegal since we are a state-funded institution. 

The irony here is, because of Mr. Riley's actions, numerous homosexuals at
our college may be cut off from a valuable communications medium while
Chuck Whealton, who has accounts at other Universities as well as a
commercial site, will not be silenced. 

The other irony is that I personally do my best to encourage equal rights
to gays. My father, as a minister, works with various religious PACs, etc,
to deny those rights.  There are numerous groups out there that are hell
bent on silencing homosexuality and shoving it back into the closet.  I
find it very sad, as an administrator who is very supportive of gay
rights, that I am often a target of abuse by readers on alt.homosexual who
are upset that one of my users is very vocal about his opinion of
homosexuality. 

