>Of course, it depends on the context.  In the case we're discussing, 
>my understanding is that the picture of a monkey in a classroom was
>labeled "Intuitively Obvious", and that this phrase, at MIT, is the
>name of an orientation program for minority students.  If this is
>all true, then I think there's a case to be made.  Neither I nor anybody 
>else is claiming that *all* monkeys are racist representations of blacks; 
>only that *some* can be.

	Your understanding in not accurate. The picture is available
in the archives of the MIT Student Association for Freedom of Expression,
now available over the network on the World-Wide-Web. In general,
URL http://www.mit.edu:8001/activities/safe/home.html, this case is at
URL http://www.mit.edu:8001/activities/safe/safe/cases/mit-pic-book/
        Or at least, it will be available until someone get threatened
strongly enough ... :-(
	Anyway, according to an article in the local paper _The Tech_:

   The original cover shows a monkey wearing glasses, dressed in a cap
   and a lab coat with a pocket protector, and holding a calculator. The
   monkey has one arm raised and is asking a question: "What does
   `intuitively obvious' mean?!"

	"Intuitively Obvious" is a *very* common phrase around MIT.
Because it's a colloquial phrase, it was also used as the the name of
three different videos about what it's like to be Black, Hispanic, or
Asian at MIT. These videos are extremely obscure, apparently known only
to the administration, a few activists, and political junkies. I'd
estimate > 95% of the MIT population has never heard of them. And I was
informed first-hand by one of the people involved with making the
picture that they didn't know about the title of the videos.

	The very disturbing thing about this incident, which prompted me
to post the message that started this thread, is the reasoning displayed
by the president of MIT in suppressing the picture. I wasn't looking for
an argument about whether his action should be technically described as
"censorship" or not. The focus should instead be on the attitude
displayed towards expression. As the article stated, ``President Vest
stopped the distribution of the picture books with the original cover
because the cover "could be misinterpreted as racially derogatory."''.
He seemed to be saying that if there was any way to connect an
expression with a racial meaning, no matter how contorted, that
expression would be treated as such. This is exactly the sort of theory
that led to the infamous "water buffalo" case at the University of
Pennsylvania. It's not a good position for a university leader to have.

	Given the concern over speech codes and overbroad harassment
policies, what he did was particularly troubling. If it is a signal of
things to come, it needs to be vigorously opposed.

