Sten Drescher <smd@floyd.brooks.af.mil> wrote:
>
>CERT> Anyone using this system expressly consents to such monitoring
>
>	Think about it for a bit.  Without monitoring apparently
>authorized users, how is a sysadmin to determine that an unauthorized
>user is using an account?  Keeping a record of (failed) attempts to
>access/modify system files or those of other users, attempts to obtain
>administrator privliges, etc, is monitoring.  

I'd suggest that the definition of "monitoring" is going to vary somewhat
on each system.  On a Unix system, for instance, should use of the "ps -eaf"
("ps aux" for Berkeleyish versions) command be considered verboten?  It
doesn't require any special privileges - any user can execute it - so I
would not consider this excessive.

My personal borderline for "monitoring" comes when someone has to use 
administrative privileges to gather information.  If you have to be
root (or SYSTEM, or OPERATOR...) to do it, I believe that some formal
procedure(s) should be observed.

>Now, observing/recording
>every keystroke entered by a user should be limited to situations where
>there is 'probable cause' to believe that an account has been
>compromised.  

Righty-o!  If, for instance, ps tells me that user xxyyy0392 is sitting
over in /usr/lanman/apps/wp51 *and* is running xmodem (a file transfer
utility), I'd take that as a fairly good indicator that I should check
and see what files xmodem is trying to upload/download.

In conclusion, I believe that some observational tools are necessary for
proper system administration.  These tools may indicate that a particular
user (or usage) bears further scrutiny; at that point, the appropriate
policies/procedures/chain-of-command should kick into full gear before 
more invasive monitoring is initiated.

--Wes

